On nested finite generators, the flattening combinator relates the union of the power-domain up-sets of the inner unions to the union of the up-sets of a list exactly when every neighbourhood in every inner list is contained in some neighbourhood of the outer list.

A generator union is a member of the source union, and a generator is a member of it, so the relation supplies a covering neighbourhood, which lies under a generator of the target. Conversely, the source is the power-domain neighbourhood generated by the list of inner unions, by comparing the mapped-list union with the indexed union. A member then lies under some inner union, a neighbourhood inside it lies under a generator, and the hypothesis supplies a covering generator whose inclusion composes with that containment.
